  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Monday: A system is slowly pushing through the \nregion with a southwesterly upper level jet support. It is \nbringing a decent plume of moisture into the Inland Northwest. \nPrecipitable water values will range from 0.6-0.8 of an inch. \nRain is likely through the day minus the Central Basin area. The\nmountain areas have a 25-75% probability of 0.2-0.5 of an inch.\nThe Eastern Basin and valleys range from 0.1-0.4 of an inch. \nNorth Cascades has the only snow chances with a 1-3 inch range \nthrough Monday morning. Breezy southerly winds across the \nColumbia Basin and up the Okanogan Valley with sustained speeds \nof 15-20 mph and gusts up to 30 mph Sunday afternoon. Highs will\nbe in the 40s to low 50s. Lows will be in the 30s.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday: A shallow ridge will bring a brief break\nfrom the showery weather. The region will be under a dry, \nstable air mass. The region will have a cooling trend. It will \nbe short lived as the next low begins to slide south of the \nregion late Wednesday. Highs will be in the 40s. Overnight lows \nwill be in the upper 20s and 30s.\n\nThursday through Saturday: The upper level jet continues to \nsteer the bulk of moisture and energy from the low south of the\nPacific Northwest. Ensembles are in decent agreement with the \ntrack. Any precip will be in the Idaho Panhandle and extreme \nEastern Washington through Thursday. Snow levels will dip to \n3000 ft allowing for a chance of snow in these areas. Little to \nno accumulation is expected. As this low exits on Friday, \nanother one will be right on its heels for Saturday. It is \ntracking more north and could bring decent precip to the \nregion. Confidence is low on any amounts and timing. Highs \ncontinue to be in the 40s. Overnight lows will be in the upper \n20s and 30s. /JDC",
